  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Given that $D=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{2}-2x?$",
  "worked_solution": "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =(x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})({x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}-2x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-2)+(2x-2) \\\\  & =2x+2x-2 \\\\  & =4x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
